title: 'https without IO::Async::SSL: no up-front check, later requests can hang'
updated: 2026-10-13T19:50:24Z
---

`IO::Async::SSL` is only a `recommends` in `cpanfile`, and `lib/Net/Async/Forgejo/API.pm` never checks for it. `Net::Async::MikroTik` documents and guards exactly this case (`p5-net-async-mikrotik/lib/Net/Async/MikroTik.pm:337-351`): Net::Async::HTTP loads IO::Async::SSL only while connecting, and a load failure there leaks the connection slot - the first request fails, every later request to that host hangs. With `max_connections_per_host => 0` here the hang may not occur, but the first failure is still an opaque `Can't locate IO/Async/SSL.pm` from inside the transport. Not reproduced here (IO::Async::SSL is installed on this machine).

Done when: an https `url` without a loadable IO::Async::SSL fails the Future at once with a message naming the module (same wording as the MikroTik sibling), and the behaviour is covered by a test that hides the module.

